From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 987: a CMDU that ieee1905d refused must say so

986 taught send_cmdu() that a message id of zero means the message never went
out. It left the branch directly above it alone, and that is the branch that
fires when the transmit is refused outright: send_cmdu_ubus() returns a negative
value, the reason goes to dbg(), and dbg() is below the default log level. The
caller gets 0xffff and reports failure, but nothing anywhere says why, so the
first thing anyone debugging this has to do is rediscover it.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. The mesh had formed as a chain,
controller <- x8 <- 8g, after a simultaneous restart of all three nodes. Asking
ieee1905d directly, bypassing map-controller entirely:

  ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:ae"}'   (8g, two hops)
    -> { "status": "fail" }
  ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"e6:a9:d2:c1:98:3f"}'   (x8, one hop)
    -> { "status": "ok", "mid": 63297 }

So ieee1905d will not address a node that is not a direct 1905 neighbour, and
map-controller is asking correctly. The refusal is one layer down. This patch
does not fix that - it makes it audible.

Why that is worth a patch of its own: the far node stays in the controller's
topology the whole time, listed with depth -1, while every query addressed to it
is dropped here. From the outside the controller looks idle rather than blocked,
and the same investigation had already been run twice against the wrong layer -
first against the agent (988, which drops CMDUs from an unknown origin) and then
against the mid == 0 path (986). Neither can fire in this state. Both were
written because the failure was silent.

Relayed multicast does reach the far node through the intermediary - 36 frames
of AP-Autoconfiguration Search carrying its AL-MAC were captured arriving at the
controller in the same run - so a path exists. Using it for unicast is a change
in ieee1905d and a design decision about where the topology's source of truth
lives; it does not belong in a logging fix.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-31 09:45:18.434895889 +0200
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-31 09:45:18.435270520 +0200
@@ -1087,7 +1087,32 @@
 
 	ret = send_cmdu_ubus(a, cmdu);
 	if (ret < 0) {
-		dbg("fail to send cmdu %04x over ubus\n", cmdu_get_type(cmdu));
+		/* ieee1905d refused to transmit.
+		 *
+		 * This is the sibling of the mid == 0 case handled below, and
+		 * it is the one that stays quiet. The message never reaches the
+		 * wire, send_cmdu() returns 0xffff, every caller reports failure
+		 * upwards - and dbg() keeps the reason out of the log at the
+		 * default level, so from the outside the controller simply does
+		 * nothing and says nothing about it.
+		 *
+		 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-31. With the mesh formed as a
+		 * chain, controller <- x8 <- 8g, asking ieee1905d directly and
+		 * bypassing this code entirely:
+		 *
+		 *   ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"<8g al-mac>"}'
+		 *     -> { "status": "fail" }
+		 *   ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"<x8 al-mac>"}'
+		 *     -> { "status": "ok", "mid": 63297 }
+		 *
+		 * ieee1905d will not address a node that is not a direct 1905
+		 * neighbour. The controller is asking correctly; the refusal is
+		 * one layer down. That is worth a line in the log, because the
+		 * node stays in the topology while every query to it is dropped
+		 * here, and nothing else anywhere says why.
+		 */
+		err("%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT " REJECTED by ieee1905d (ret %d) - destination is not a direct 1905 neighbour\n",
+		    __func__, cmdu_get_type(cmdu), MAC2STR(cmdu->origin), ret);
 		goto error;
 	}
 
